        %h2= crsf_h2_why
        %p
          Most ExpressLRS receivers — <strong>HappyModel EP2</strong>, <strong>EP1</strong>, <strong>EPW6</strong> in CRSF mode — expose only a serial <code>CRSF</code> stream and no usable PWM headers. PteronautOS expects three servo channels (left wing, right wing, crest rudder). The cleanest, cheapest bridge is a tiny <strong>ESP32-S3</strong> board programmed as a pure <em>CRSF→PWM converter</em>: it reads the CRSF frames over UART and fans out rock-steady servo pulses.
        %p
          The <strong>Waveshare ESP32-S3-Tiny</strong> (~6&nbsp;USD) is ideal: it is 23&nbsp;×&nbsp;18&nbsp;mm, weighs under 3&nbsp;g without headers, runs at up to 240&nbsp;MHz, and — unlike the ESP8266 — every GPIO is a true PWM-capable pin. A single board can generate up to 8 servo channels.

        %h2= crsf_h2_wiring
        %table
          %tr
            %th RX (EP2 / CRSF)
            %th ESP32-S3-Tiny
            %th Note
          %tr
            %td TX (CRSF out)
            %td GPIO44 (U0RX)
            %td Cross: RX←TX
          %tr
            %td RX (CRSF in)
            %td GPIO43 (U0TX)
            %td Cross: TX←RX
          %tr
            %td 5V
            %td 5V
            %td Shared power rail
          %tr
            %td GND
            %td GND
            %td Common ground (mandatory)
        %p
          CRSF runs at <strong>420&nbsp;000&nbsp;baud</strong> (ELRS) — do not use the default 115&nbsp;200. Servos connect to any three free GPIOs, e.g. <code>GPIO1</code> (left wing), <code>GPIO2</code> (right wing), <code>GPIO3</code> (rudder), each with its own signal line; servo <code>5V</code> and <code>GND</code> come from a shared BEC rail.

        %h2= crsf_h2_flash
        %p
          Flash the converter with a minimal Arduino sketch. The core loop is a CRSF parser that maps the first four channels (roll, pitch, throttle, yaw) to 988–2012&nbsp;µs servo pulses:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              #include <ESP32Servo.h>

              Servo wingL, wingR, rudder;
              #define CRSF_BAUD 420000
              #define CRSF_MAX_CH 16
              uint16_t channels[CRSF_MAX_CH];

              void setup() {
                Serial.begin(CRSF_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1);
                wingL.attach(1); wingR.attach(2); rudder.attach(3);
                wingL.writeMicroseconds(1500);
                wingR.writeMicroseconds(1500);
                rudder.writeMicroseconds(1500);
              }

              void loop() {
                if (Serial.available() >= 26) {
                  uint8_t hdr = Serial.read();
                  if (hdr == 0xC8 || hdr == 0xEE) {          // device / extended
                    uint8_t len = Serial.read();
                    uint8_t type = Serial.read();
                    if (type == 0x16) {                       // RC channels
                      uint8_t payload[22];
                      Serial.readBytes(payload, 22);
                      for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) {
                        channels[i] = (payload[i*11/8] | payload[i*11/8+1]<<8)
                                      & 0x07FF;
                      }
                      wingR.writeMicroseconds(map(channels[0],172,1811,988,2012));
                      rudder.writeMicroseconds(map(channels[1],172,1811,988,2012));
                      wingL.writeMicroseconds(map(channels[2],172,1811,988,2012));
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
        %p
          A production-ready version validates the CRC, frames the stream on the 26-byte packet boundary and keeps a failsafe (if no valid frame arrives within 1&nbsp;s, hold the last known pulses or centre to 1500&nbsp;µs). PteronautOS itself already implements a centred failsafe on the RX — but a standalone converter should be fail-safe on its own.
        %p
          A ready-to-compile, CRC-checked implementation with failsafe and up to eight channels lives in the repository as
          <a href="https://github.com/dantiel/PteronautOS/blob/master/sketches/esp32s3_yoshimitsu/esp32s3_yoshimitsu.ino">sketches/esp32s3_yoshimitsu/esp32s3_yoshimitsu.ino</a>.
          If you also want to flash EP2-class receivers without an FTDI adapter, the same S3 board can become a pocket flasher —
          see <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}tutorials/esp32s3-toolbox/">Tutorial 07 — ESP32-S3 Toolbox</a>.

        %h2= crsf_h2_verify
        %ul
          %li Power the RX and the ESP32-S3 from the same 5V rail.
          %li On the radio, bind and wiggle each stick; the servos must track smoothly with no jitter.
          %li Verify the failsafe: switch the transmitter off — servos must return to 1500&nbsp;µs.
          %li If pulses are stuck or twitchy, confirm the baud is 420&nbsp;000 and GND is shared.

        %p.hw-note
          = hw_ep2_note
          %a{href: "#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}tutorials/crsf-converter/"} → tutorial
